  "kind": "quirk",
  "title": "Punctuation before a Japanese repeat mark sorts one way after a Latin letter, the other after Han",
  "statement": "Comparing a string with punctuation before a repeat or prolonged-sound mark against the same string without the punctuation, the order depends on the character at the start. After a Latin letter the punctuated string is smaller: \"a-ゝx\" < \"aゝx\". After a Han character it is larger: \"時-ゝx\" > \"時ゝx\". This held for every pair read, with the marks ゝ, 々 and ー and with hyphen-minus, U+2212 and U+2019 as the punctuation. In all 66 readings the two strings are never equal, and a string with the mark added sorts after the string without it, after Latin and after Han alike: \"a−ゝ\" > \"a−\".",
  "status": "measured",
  "basis": "66 recorded readings of =, < and > on 22 pairs of strings, 11 pairs after 時 and 11 after a.",
  "inferred": null,
  "documentation": null,
